  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Nor'easter continues to bring gusty winds and \nperiods of rain to central Pennsylvania this weekend.\n\nA strong Nor'easter (988 mb) off the coast of Delaware coupled \nwith a broad area of high pressure stationed to the north will \ncontinue to bring gusty winds and periods of rain throughout the\nweekend across central Pennsylvania. So far across central \nPennsylvania gusts have remained largely below 30 mph; however, \nthere have been some gusts that have briefly gusted above that \nthreshold such as Lancaster Airport (KLNS), where a 33 mph wind \ngust was observed this morning. In terms of precipitation, \nperiods of rain likely continue across portions of eastern \nPennsylvania, with a brief area of relatively dry conditions \nbeginning to fill in behind this precipitation. Guidance \ncontinues to outline a general trend in continued periods of \nrain through the evening hours before a brief lull in \nprecipitation is observed later this evening and ahead of \nsunrise on Sunday. Further west, low-level dry air continues to \nerode rainfall as it dived southwestward. This trend likely \ncontinues with recent observations at JST/AOO/HMZ outlining \n30-40% RH values although slightly more saturation is projected \nbased on all model guidance to occur this evening.\n\nAs the nor'easter tracks closer to the NJ Coast, an increase in\nwinds is expected to occur across much of the forecast area, \nwith widespread 25-30 mph gusts on Sunday. Some locations across\neastern Pennsylvania, especially areas with more complex \nterrain will likely push closer to 35-40 mph with localized \ngusts pushing close to Wind Advisory criteria. In terms of Wind \nAdvisories, there was some consideration on hoisting flags for \nSullivan, Schuylkill, Lebanon, and Lancaster Counties; however, \nconfidence remains too low at this time with NBM 24-hr Max Gusts\nonly showing a 20-40% chance for exceeding criteria. That being\nsaid, with leaves remaining on trees across the region this \ntime of year, cannot rule out some tree damage and isolated \npower outages on Sunday. Another aspect of the closer track of \nthe storm will be more widespread rainfall; however, no flooding\nconcerns are expected with the stratiform nature of this rain \nas storm-total rainfall approaches one inch across Sullivan and \nLancaster Counties.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Tranquil conditions expected for the first half \nof next week with a slight warming trend emerging across the \nregion.\n\nAs the aforementioned nor'easter shifts further north and east\nof the region, the bulk of precipitation is expected to end by\nMonday morning outside of some lingering showers across eastern\nPennsylvania. Once these lingering showers begin to diminish, \nfairly high (~70-80%) confidence in dry conditions prevailing \nthrough Wednesday. All model guidance trends towards a warming \ntrend in this timeframe, with above-average temperatures \nprevailing area wide by Tuesday. Unsettled pattern begins to \nemerge towards the end of the week with a return of rain \nchances; however, model differences bring low confidence to the \nforecast this far out in the game.",
